#. Performing ', Edit...'
#. Showing dialog 3 (@Z9MM87) '@SNUKQZ - Edit Property' (owner: @U8KDDP)
#. Performing ', Cancel'
#. Performing ', Edit...'
#. Showing dialog 4 (@DH747U) '@W3A13L - Edit Property' (owner: @U8KDDP)
#. Performing ', Cancel'
msgctxt "lblPropertyDescription:"
msgid "By default, SmartGit is rather conservative when updating submodules: for instance modified submodules won't be touched, even if the registered commit in the parent repository has changed. Sometimes it may be convenient to still 'update' these submodules, e.g. by merging local commits with the changed, registered commit. This is what `git submodule update` will do, if key `submodule.\\$name.update` is properly configured in `.git\\\\/config` of the parent repository. To invoke `git submodule update` for modified submodules, set this value to `true`."
msgstr ""

#. Showing dialog 1 (@U8KDDP) '@HT06E6 - Preferences' (owner: @UZ2V0R)
#. Performing ', Edit...'
#. Showing dialog 2 (@EN0MD9) '@IBD2AW - Edit Property' (owner: @U8KDDP)
#. Performing ', Cancel'
#. Performing ', Edit...'
#. Showing dialog 3 (@Z9MM87) '@SNUKQZ - Edit Property' (owner: @U8KDDP)
#. Performing ', Cancel'
#. Performing ', Edit...'
#. Showing dialog 4 (@DH747U) '@W3A13L - Edit Property' (owner: @U8KDDP)
#. Performing ', Cancel'
msgctxt "lblPropertyDescription:"
msgid "By default, SmartGit suggests all resolvables issues for resolution which could be extracted from the commit message, regardless of the exact commit message text. When working on large issues and pushing several commits for a single issue, this may be annoying. In this case you may tell SmartGit to scan the commit message for specific /work-in-progress/ markers, like `WIP`. If such markers are found, no issue IDs will be extracted from the commit message. Use comma \\\\\\(`,`\\\\\\) to separate markers. Upper\\\\/lower case matters.\\n\\nExample:\\n\\n`WIP, work-in-progress, \\\\\\(todo\\\\\\)`"
msgstr ""

